All observations were made at magnifications 6–288× on a Leica MS 5 or a Leica M165 C microscope. We examined a suite of morphological characters commonly used in ant systematics such as pilosity, pubescence, colour, shape of the head, shape of the petiole and mandibular dentition. In addition, we quantified several standard morphometric characters using an ocular micrometre. Since the smaller Dorylus workers are less differentiated between species (Schöning et al. 2005b) and subcastes can only be categorised in an arbitrary manner (see Fig. 2), we decided to use only workers with a HW larger than an arbitrary cut-off value of 1.5 mm for the morphometric analyses. We took measurements for up to six individuals per sample. The characters examined were:

